# Design: the engine of blueprints, recipes and cells. The page spec/nums.md is the authority; these nodes say what it chose and why.

n: 5.19
name: the-engine
kind: hub
is: What the seed stores and runs: blueprints, recipes and cells in one numeric space at the altitude of programming languages, as NUMS has them, executed as they stand. Words and names are tables beside the nodes, and a language is a lens that writes text from them.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.1
name: stored-is-meaning-at-language-altitude
kind: decision
is: What is stored is numeric and language neutral, NUMS nodes with a separate symbol table. Blueprints can be classes, structs, lists, maps, unions, generics and templates; recipes can be generic or templates, and operators are special recipes. Any language translates in without losing its semantics and out without becoming low level, and objects run directly, with no lowering.
status: given
see: 3.1 3.2 5.1.1 5.2.4
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.2
name: a-full-engine
kind: decision
is: The engine makes cells from blueprints with constructor recipes and runs any recipe on any cells. Cells are boxed and unboxed with an implicit blueprint link; values cast and convert among all native and model layouts of integers and floats; structs, classes, templates, generics and genetics are supported in full. The design comes first, then the implementation as nodes, which a lens shows in BML, Go or any language, its text cached locally and not stored.
status: given
see: 5.19.1
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.3
name: the-root-is-the-package
kind: decision
is: A catalogue entry is package.level.type.instance packed in one number: instance in bits 0 to 31, type 32 to 47, level 48 to 55, package 56 to 63. Package 0 is the bootstrap's own kinds, 1 blueprints, 2 recipes, 3 cells, 16 the engine's own definitions, 17 and up a language or module. The number is a catalogue entry, up to 2^63 - 1. A composite has no coordinate: its identity is its content id.
status: inferred
from: 5.2.1 5.2.6 3.1.2 3.1.9.1 5.19.1
conf: mid
why: Axiom 2 names the four parts. NUMS tells blueprint ids from recipe ids only by a Go type and mints composites by arrival order, which two parties cannot share; one number that says its root, and content for everything made, keeps the shape and drops both faults.
flip: Seven bits of package and eight of level are narrower than NUMS's sixteen.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.4
name: the-category-is-the-recipe-applied
kind: decision
is: The category of a recipe node is the recipe that is applied: a catalogue entry, an instance of one for given blueprints, or a declared function. Plus on two signed 32-bit integers is a node under (Instance Plus i32); a call is a node under the function's declaration. So an operator is a recipe like any other, a generic recipe is a template with holes, and the blueprint of every use follows from the node without running it.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.1 5.5.5 5.1.1 3.2.8.3
conf: mid
why: NUMS's newest model holds a recipe template for each operator and makes every use an instance of it. With the node shape of 5.1.1 that is a category which is itself a node.
flip: An operator used without its blueprints must be resolved when it runs.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.5
name: a-number-is-a-kind-and-a-layout
kind: decision
is: A numeric blueprint is its kind and how it is held: (Integer (TwosComplement 32)), (Decimal (Ieee 64)), (Decimal (Brain 16)), (Decimal (Float8E4M3)), (Decimal (Affine (Integer (TwosComplement 8)) scale zero)). Layout is a family of the blueprint catalogue. A cast names one of five rules: exact, round, truncate, saturate, wrap. The kind without a layout is the number of a language that does not say its width.
status: inferred
from: 5.1.4 3.1.4.2 5.19.2
conf: mid
why: NUMS has one Integer and one Decimal, which cannot drive machine code or hold a model's weights. Keeping the kind and adding the layout loses nothing a language says and names every native and model format.
flip: Two layouts of one kind are different blueprints and need a cast between them.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.6
name: a-cell-is-unboxed-where-its-place-says-what-it-is
kind: decision
is: A value whose blueprint has a fixed layout is held as its bytes in its place, a local's in a frame, a member's in an object, an element's in a list, and the blueprint is that of the place, not stored again. A value whose place does not fix its blueprint, Any, an interface, a union, is held as a blueprint and a payload. An allocated object is a record of the region and starts with its blueprint and writer.
status: inferred
from: 5.7.6 5.7.7 5.5.1 5.19.2
conf: mid
why: Cells are both fully boxed and fully unboxed with an implicit blueprint link (5.19.2). In ground/nums-cells.ck the constructor and two views read one room, and a failed field write restores its bytes.
flip: Reading a part needs the holder's blueprint in hand; a value passed as Any is boxed on the way.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.7
name: a-blueprint-makes-its-cells
kind: decision
is: An object blueprint is its parts: members, each a blueprint with attributes on the reference and an init recipe, and methods, each a signature and a body. Its layout is computed from it, the parents' members composed in. new takes room for the layout, runs each member's init, then the constructor with the arguments. A member is read by (Member B k), the blueprint and the place, so the node says of which shape it reads a part.
status: inferred
from: 5.5.4 5.5.7 3.1.2.8 3.1.8.4 5.19.1
conf: mid
why: NUMS's named cell is an access recipe, a base blueprint and a constructor recipe, and its member ids pair a blueprint with a place. The seed had read parts by bare numbers.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.8
name: run-as-written
kind: decision
is: The engine runs a recipe node by what its category says, on cells laid out by their blueprints. An if chooses, a loop repeats, a return leaves the frame; nothing is turned into another form first. The answer to any node is nothing, 0, 1 or a node. Each catalogue entry is bound to a recipe in form that runs it, and a node whose category is an instance runs the instance's binding; each can be replaced by code written from rows keyed by that instance. Choice, fail, commit and all are entries of the catalogue.
status: inferred
from: 5.4.1 5.4.2 5.4.6 5.1.2 5.19.1
conf: mid
why: Objects run directly, with no lowering (5.19.1). The seed had stored an if as four core nodes and run those.
flip: What form cannot yet do, such as writing bytes in place, becomes an operation of the bootstrap; if that grows past its budget, the line moves.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.9
name: a-language-is-rows-and-its-text-is-not-stored
kind: decision
is: A lens for a language is rows: a template with numbered places for each catalogue entry, a spelling for each blueprint, the forms of a declaration. One engine fills them to write text; the same rows run the other way read it. Names come from symbol tables keyed by node and lens. The text a lens writes may be cached on the machine and is never in the repository.
status: inferred
from: 5.3.2 5.2.4 3.2.8.3 3.1.4.3 5.19.2
conf: mid
why: NUMS's label tree already carries such templates and one table per language. Writing comes first because it needs no grammar.
flip: The Go projection and the BML grammar read into nodes. The BML syntax tree can express declarations that the engine cannot yet execute (5.19.28).
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.10
name: variation-on-typed-nodes
kind: decision
is: A population holds immutable genomes. Development produces a phenotype and a recipe evaluates its fitness. Selection and variation operate on those observations. A candidate may occur several times even when its content is shared. A seed and the same deterministic recipes reproduce the generation.
status: inferred
from: 5.5.5 3.1.8.2 5.12.7 5.19.4
conf: mid
why: The generation in ground/nums-evolution.ck develops and runs recipe trees. The separate genetic representation and typed variation are grounded in the sources in spec/nums.md.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.11
name: the-earlier-catalogue-is-package-zero
kind: decision
is: The 75 operations the bootstrap runs today, choice, seq, child, join and the rest, are package 0. The lens, the emitter and the tools are written on them. They stay while those are written again as recipes of the new catalogue and go when nothing is left on them.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.8 7.22
conf: mid
why: What stands still runs and is how the next thing is read and stored; it is not what a language translates into.
flip: For a time the repository holds two catalogues.
at: 2026-11-26


n: 5.19.12
name: genetic-algorithms
kind: decision
is: Genetic support begins with research into how genetic code is modelled, before any operators are written.
status: given
see: 5.19.10
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.13
name: the-runtime-reads-no-words
kind: decision
is: The runtime needs no words: it loads nodes as numeric payloads and runs them by the ids of blueprints, recipes and cells, all already resolved; text appears only where something is shown to a person.
status: given
see: 5.19.1 5.19.8
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.14
name: the-generic-runtime-is-bindings
kind: decision
is: The generic runtime is the catalogue's recipes bound to recipes in form, so the walker runs a recipe node as it stands and is at that node. A call makes its frame a room under the declaration, laid out by the blueprints of its places: a fixed layout as bytes, a Bool as a byte, anything else as a node's handle. An operator finds the recipe for its instance once and keeps it under the instance node. No word and no text is in it.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.8 5.19.13 5.7.6
conf: mid
why: The walker looks up a node's recipe by number and shows where it stands. The four unsigned order relations in nums/run.ck use one word instance. The resolved image calls that instance by id before and after attaching machine code.
flip: A lazy recipe takes its parts through a call built for them, which costs a node per shape and several steps per use; the walker could give the parts itself.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.15
name: one-recipe-on-many-targets
kind: decision
is: A recipe runs on a generic runtime, or becomes machine code for the CPU or the GPU with or without tracking, tracing, boxing and unrolling, from the same nodes.
status: given
see: 5.19.13 5.19.14
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.16
name: parts-that-evolve-together
kind: decision
is: Code is made of reusable parts that interlock and can be tried against one another and evolved, not of low-level prelude as too much of the old kernel was.
status: given
see: 5.19.4 5.19.10
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.17
name: recipes-between-predictions
kind: decision
is: Code can observe, adapt and evolve itself; a recipe can stand as a token in a model's stream; blueprints are whole structures, classes, templates and generics; recipes are generic algorithms over many blueprints and cells that write code as they run.
status: given
see: 5.19.1 5.19.16
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.18
name: a-token-can-call-a-resident-instance
kind: decision
is: A vocabulary maps a numeric token to a target id. Its call takes ordinary cell ids. The instance can accept another recipe or attach code while its callers keep their target. A trial of a different body uses the same task and observes its result before the choice restores cell writes and bindings.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.4 5.19.10 5.19.16 5.19.17
src: 2.7.36 2.7.37
conf: mid
why: A token can use the existing numeric port. In the image the word subtraction instance serves typed callers and a vocabulary slot before and after compilation.
limit: The example supplies a token directly. A live model decoder and selection across hardware targets are open.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.19
name: the-kernel-solved-it-in-text
kind: decision
is: Most technical problems were solved in the coherence-kernel, which stored text where this body stores numeric nodes; its solutions are carried over as nodes.
status: given
see: 5.19.1 5.19.15
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.20
name: only-final-numeric-code
kind: decision
is: Only final, end-to-end numeric node code is committed, with no bands, doors, gates or checks added as static code.
status: given
see: 5.19.16 5.19.17 7.13
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.21
name: floats-are-one-recipe
kind: decision
is: Arithmetic on floats is one recipe for every layout: the operands are taken apart into sign, significand, power and special, the result is computed exactly, kept to 62 bits with every bit beyond folded into the last one, and rounded to nearest even by the layout's own encoder. Hardware is a faster form of that recipe for the layouts a processor has, and never its meaning.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.2 5.19.5 5.19.17
conf: mid
why: Rounding a result kept to odd at two bits more than the target gives the result rounded once, so one exact path serves binary64 and E4M3 alike. It matched the M4's binary16, 32 and 64 hardware bit for bit in a sweep, and the layouts the M4 lacks have no other meaning to stand on.
flip: An exact path over byte magnitudes is slow, and a layout used in bulk without hardware needs a faster form of its own, a table or code written for it.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.22
name: machine-code-from-rules-and-rows
kind: decision
is: The compiler is a code rule for each category of the catalogue, kept under the category as the runtime's binding is, over the target as rows (5.19.31). What an operator is in machine words is a row of templates whose holes are named roles. Places stay unboxed in registers; where the runtime would answer nothing the code declines and the call runs on frames.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.15 5.19.19 5.19.16 5.19.14
conf: mid
why: The kernel's encoder was a table of base words and field multipliers, and every word it wrote was byte-verified; here the table is nodes, so a new instruction, operator or register is a row and not a definition. A rule per category is the runtime's own dispatch, so the two cannot drift apart in what they cover. Observed: a review that found the frame and the door written as raw numbers in five places, and branches counted by hand, led to the target as rows and labels; the sweep of 5.19.29 holds the result.
flip: Registers are given in order and a call saves every value in flight; a search over allocations and unrolling factors, scored by time, would choose better than one fixed rule.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.23
name: the-engine-is-one-layer
kind: decision
is: The runtime, the float definition, the machine's rows and the compiler are bound in one layer, and each writes its tables once the layer stands.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.14 5.19.22
conf: high
why: A recipe handed to a definition runs on the bindings that definition sees. Observed: a recipe given to an older helper answered its own body as data, because the newer definition it named was not there for it.
flip: A helper that ran what it is handed where it was asked would let the parts be layers of their own.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.24
name: a-function-and-what-it-calls-are-one-leaf
kind: decision
is: A declaration is compiled together with every declaration it calls into one code leaf, each with its own frame and a call a branch between them. A callee that does not compile fails the leaf. A new declaration of an entry makes every leaf compiled before it compile again at its next call.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.15 5.19.22
conf: mid
why: Each code leaf is copied into the code region on its own, so one leaf cannot branch into another; a leaf that holds what it calls needs no address resolved while it runs, and recursion through two declarations stays in machine code. The kernel's emitter compiled a module of definitions with what they call to one leaf. Observed: even and odd through two entries stay in one leaf, and a million calls of a second declaration in a loop took about 1 ms beyond loading and compiling.
flip: A leaf per declaration, called through a table the runtime keeps, would share code between callers and let a declaration be replaced without compiling its callers again.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.25
name: a-cast-is-a-row-of-rounding-and-range
kind: decision
is: Each of the five cast rules is a row of how it rounds and what it does beyond the target's range. Compiled code does that on the hardware where the hardware's answer is the runtime's, and declines where it is not.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.21 5.19.22
conf: mid
why: Exact, nearest and truncate answer nothing beyond the range, saturate clamps and wrap keeps the low bits, in the runtime's own recipe for a cast. Binary64 holds ±2^63 and 2^64 exactly, so a float is bounded there before it becomes an integer. Observed: every cast between nine layouts by all five rules over fourteen boundary values answered the same compiled and on frames all the way down (5.19.29).
flip: A conversion the hardware does otherwise, a NaN's payload or an infinity under saturate, could be done in machine code by more instructions instead of declining.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.26
name: a-part-that-does-not-lower-fails-the-whole
kind: decision
is: A code rule binds every part it builds before it puts the words together, so a part that does not lower fails the function and never leaves a gap in its code.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.22
conf: high
why: A tuple gathers the answers of its parts with all, which keeps only answers that are something: a part that fails or answers nothing gives none. Observed in a cast sweep: a conversion the compiler could not write dropped out of the code and the function returned the wrong register. The same shape stood in reading a deep temporary, storing, returning, passing arguments and taking a callee's answer.
flip: A builder of lists that failed with any part would let the rules build their words in place.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.27
name: bmf-character-cursor-and-parser-control
kind: decision
is: The BML parser runs on BMF, and BMF's cursor moves over characters, not lines. The parser is written with choice, cut and stop, as the thesis wrote it; those primitives were made for it.
status: given
see: 3.5.4.1 3.5.4.2
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.28
name: bml-reads-by-running-bmf
kind: decision
is: The thesis and original JBML grammar supply the BML productions. A form.bml section selects the later kernel's productions. The same BMF matcher runs both the BML grammar and the grammar a BML program defines. A character cursor and native Form choices carry the search across lines. Fail resumes a branch, crossing Cut ends the search and Stop ends it on entry. Rule and argument references are numbers when the linked grammar runs.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.28.1 5.19.27 5.19.9 3.5.4.1 3.5.4.2 3.5.4.3
src: 2.3.1 2.8.6 2.8.12 2.8.14 2.30.17
conf: high
why: ground/bmf-sweep.ck compares direct and specialized matches with independently enumerated spellings, capture trees and cursor positions. ground/bmf-items.ck installs a grammar-owned class and restores a capture action's write on failure. ground/nums-lenses.ck runs the same item methods on BML, and the numeric port runs a generic rule by id.
limit: BML links named and anonymous captures. Capture actions run through the numeric API. BML action binding and literal whitespace annotations remain open. A Java view of the range class has been compiled and compared with Seed. Projection of the whole search package is open.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.28.1
name: bmf-as-the-shared-parser
kind: decision
is: Please reimplements similar to original java and improve what you can, until we have a fully reusable BMF implementation, that is the cornerstone of all grammar parsers, so having that solid and generic is the highest priority
status: given
by: owner
see: 5.19.27
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.29
name: compiled-code-is-held-to-frames-all-the-way-down
kind: decision
is: Compiled code is held to the runtime by a ground program that runs each case both ways, the reference on frames all the way down, and answers the cases whose outcomes differ, with how many compiled and how many the machine code answered itself. The cases are every operator and cast over boundary values, through a call, and the edges of a call: arguments outside their layout, returns between widths, bools, a continue in a do loop, a literal beyond 64 bits, an argument that changes a local, a nothing as a condition and as an argument.
status: observed
from: 5.19.22 5.19.24 5.19.25 5.19.26
src: 2.30.18
conf: high
why: A reference that runs its callees compiled cannot see a callee's defect, and a sweep that is not committed holds nothing. Counting what compiled and what the machine answered shows the comparison did not pass by declining. Observed on 2026-11-26: a sweep that varied values only inside their own class answered none differing while a lattice over the classes, int and Bool among them, found 398 of 3,556 cases differing; widened along the classes, every value kind and the literal forms, it runs 37,822 cases, none differ, 30,269 compiled and 27,108 were answered by the machine code. Running it found two defects in the reference itself (5.19.30) and one in the compiler, a whole number zero-extended whatever its sign.
flip: A defect the reference and the compiler share would agree with itself; a third reading, the hardware's own answer or a definition from outside, would see it.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.30
name: on-frames-a-nothing-ends-the-call
kind: decision
is: On frames a nothing ends the call with no answer, wherever it stands: returned, passed, written, tested or left as a statement. A call's arguments are evaluated one after another, and each keeps what it changed.
status: observed
from: 5.19.14 5.19.26 5.19.29
src: 2.30.18
conf: high
why: all keeps only answers that are something and undoes what was done inside it. Observed on 2026-11-26: a return built with a tuple of a nothing answered yes, so 5/0 returned yes on frames; and arguments evaluated under all lost their changes, so g(i++) left i where it was on frames and one higher in machine code. With one check that fails on a nothing and keeps its reason, a division by zero now answers nothing with the reason zero both ways.
flip: A nothing carried out as a value, as a NaN is, would let a caller decide what it means; C leaves such a program undefined.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.31
name: the-target-and-the-families-are-rows
kind: decision
is: The target is rows: each instruction an entry with its base word and its fields, each field a position, a width and a kind the packer checks; the condition codes with their inverses; which register does what. Each family of values, whole, real and truth, is a row of templates, and what a family admits, which operators and which relations, is rows. Code is laid out with fresh labels and jumps that name them. Where there is more than one way, ordered choice takes the first that holds.
status: inferred
from: 5.19.22 7.24
conf: mid
why: A fact written once cannot disagree with itself. Observed: the frame, the door and the result kinds had stood as raw numbers in several definitions and branches were counted by hand; as rows and labels, nothing but the rows says them.
flip: Rows chosen for one processor may hide what another needs, a register pair or a condition the first lacks.
at: 2026-11-26

n: 5.19.32
name: one-recipe-says-what-a-slot-holds
kind: decision
is: What a slot of a layout holds for a value is one recipe, and both sides use it: frames when they write a parameter or a local, the machine's entry when it hands an argument to code. An integer slot holds the value's number, yes as 1, no as 0 and a typed integer by its own sign, kept to the slot's width; a Bool holds 0 or 1; a float slot holds the value converted by value; what no slot of that layout takes fails the call.
status: observed
from: 5.19.29 5.19.30 7.24
src: 2.30.18
conf: high
why: Each side had its own rule, and they disagreed where a value changes class. Observed on 2026-11-26: yes passed to a u8 became 33, its catalogue number, on frames and 1 in code; an i8 holding -1 passed to an i32 became 255 on frames and in compiled calls between declarations, and -1 in code entered from frames. With one recipe, and the compiled conversion extending by the source's sign, the 1,323 cases that pass each kind of value to each class agree.
flip: A value that no slot of its layout takes could be converted by a rule of the language instead of failing; C converts a float to an integer by truncation.
at: 2026-11-26
